          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Anxiety sits in the mental and behavioural chapter. Where would a physical symptom of it, like palpitations, be classified?",
          "answer": "**Where anxiety sits:** The retrieved passages do not cover the F40-F48 anxiety block. They only describe the start of Chapter 5 (F01-F99, mental, behavioral and neurodevelopmental disorders), which every F code belongs to. I could not confirm the anxiety codes from the corpus, so please check them in the official ICD-10-CM tabular list.\n\n**Where palpitations sit:** Palpitations have their own code, R00.2, in Chapter 18 (symptoms, signs and abnormal findings, R00-R99). The Chapter 18 summary says symptoms and signs of the circulatory and respiratory systems open at R00 to R09, and lists palpitations among them (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 18: symptoms, signs and abnormal findings*). The circulatory chapter summary adds that palpitations, chest pain and similar findings \"stay with the symptoms and abnormal findings in Chapter 18 until a diagnosis is recorded\" (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 9: diseases of the circulatory system*).\n\n**Coding a symptom alongside a diagnosis:** The retrieved passages show a general pattern, but not the full rule:\n- A symptom with no established diagnosis is coded in Chapter 18.\n- Some symptoms are not coded when a related condition is documented, because the condition already includes them. The asthma summary gives the example that wheeze, cough and shortness of breath are not coded when asthma is documented, because they are \"intrinsic to it\" (*Coding asthma: J45*).\n- The Chapter 18 summary also says some symptom codes are reported in addition to a diagnosis because they \"carry information the diagnosis does not.\" That passage was cut off, so I cannot give the exact conditions.\n\nI could not retrieve the specific guidance on whether palpitations would be coded alongside an anxiety diagnosis, because my lookup budget ran out. A coder or the official guidelines would need to confirm that.\n\n**Safety note:** If palpitations come with chest pain, fainting, severe shortness of breath, or feeling very unwell, get emergency medical help right away. For ongoing palpitations, talk to a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
